<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agenais</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agenois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Agenais is a historical region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, corresponding roughly to the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lot-et-garonne">Lot-et-Garonne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Part of the duchy of Aquitaine, it was given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> as the dowry of his sister Joan on her marriage to Raymond VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 1196, and it was later contested between the English and French kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agenais.jpg" alt="Agenais" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oie blanche · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_de_l%27Agenais.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
